00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:43.760
Now here is something that most retail investors get wrong.

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:47.040
And it cost me money before I figured it out.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:52.640
Imagine you added an energy name last week after seeing a normal course issue or bid filing.

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:58.920
Your fill arrived at the prevailing ask yet the real mechanics between announcement and execution

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:02.720
determine whether that capital return actually accrues to you.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:09.880
The issuer can repurchase up to a stated daily limit, often 0.5% of public float,

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:17.220
which creates a persistent bid that narrows the spread and supports price even on down days for the broader index.

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:24.200
Most retail investors assume the buyback simply reduces shares outstanding and lifts earnings per share.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:33.520
But the immediate effect is order flow absorption that can lift the stock 2-4% within the first two weeks if volume exceeds the 20-day average.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:41.040
Professionals always check the exact filing for maximum daily volume and blackout periods before sizing the position.

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:57.960
International petroleum corporation announced the repurchase of 72,282 common shares between August 6th and 7th under its previously authorized normal course issuer bid program listed on both the TSX and NASDAQ stock home.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:03.980
The concrete misconception to avoid is treating every buyback as an automatic green light.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:12.300
The fix is to confirm the program size relative to average daily volume and pair it with the same day volume spike before entering.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:24.140
